{"text": "A strong profile begins with what the student can do and what helps the student engage. The profile lists these strengths for Ethan: Strong visual reasoning; Enjoys graphic design and volleyball; Confident ASL communicator; and Careful observer of group dynamics. They give the team meaningful entry points for instruction, motivation, peer connection, and self-advocacy.\n\nHelpful preferences are: Captions always on; Clear sight lines to faces and interpreter; Written key vocabulary; and One speaker at a time. Respecting these preferences can increase participation and support student choice.\n\nCommunication access must be planned explicitly. Ethan’s communication profile includes the following: Uses ASL, speech, and text; May miss speech in noise even with devices; Needs names before questions in group talk; and Prefers direct communication, not through the interpreter. Staff should communicate directly with Ethan, allow the required processing or response time, and confirm meaning without speaking for the student. If communication becomes less effective, check access, fatigue, stress, sensory load, and the communication environment.\n\nThe key principle is to presume competence while making communication reliable. Next, I’ll explain the conditions in which Ethan learns best and the barriers that can make learning harder.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-F-03.mp3", "words": [{"text": "A", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.225, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 1}, {"text": "strong", "start": 0.2375, "end": 0.6, "start_char": 2, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "profile", "start": 0.6125, "end": 1.225, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 16}, {"text": "begins", "start": 1.2375, "end": 1.7, "start_char": 17, "end_char": 23}, {"text": "with", "start": 1.7125, "end": 1.8625, "start_char": 24, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "what", "start": 1.875, "end": 2.075, "start_char": 29, "end_char": 33}, {"text": "the", "start": 2.0875, "end": 2.1875, "start_char": 34, "end_